# Platforms on which tier T3 -- whole-file byte identity against the reference
# outputs, after normalization -- is a GATING check rather than a reported one.
#
# One platform key per line: linux, macos, or windows. Blank lines and lines
# starting with '#' are ignored.
#
# Why this file exists instead of a flag in the script. T3 is the only check
# that is tight enough to catch a wrong Lennard-Jones epsilon or sigma: those
# parameters reach nothing in the deterministic prologue, so tier T1 cannot see
# them and tier T2 would only notice a gross error. Chunks 3 and 4 of the
# release plan state their own gate as "byte-identical on the committed
# fixtures", and this is what makes that claim mean something.
#
# But T3 is also the check most likely to fail for a reason that is not physics:
# a different compiler may sum hundreds of thousands of trajectory
# contributions in a different order, and the last printed digit moves.
#
# So every platform starts listed here, as the hypothesis. If a platform turns
# out not to be byte-reproducible, removing it is a commit with a written reason
# in this file -- visible, reviewed, and dated -- instead of an invisible
# default. A gate that gets quietly weakened is worse than no gate, because
# every later change inherits its reassurance.
#
# T1 and T2 gate on every platform regardless of this file.

linux
macos
windows
